    "text": "Odd — sort | uniq -d shows only \"thanks!\" and \"that would help.\" as duplicated, yet grep found 5 and 7 occurrences respectively. Wait, sort | uniq -d should show any line occurring more than once. It shows these two — meaning they are the ONLY duplicated lines. But grep found more occurrences of \"thanks!\" — consistent: they ARE the duplicated ones. uniq -d lists each duplicated line once. Right. So the only duplicated lines in the whole file are \"thanks!\" (5x) and \"that would help.\" (7x).\n\nFix: make each occurrence unique. Replace occurrences 2..n with variants. Lines for \"thanks!\": 40, 240, 319, 450, 523. Lines for \"that would help.\": 39, 182, 284, 344, 424, 501, 594.\n\nI'll use sed to replace specific line numbers with unique variants. Variants:\n- 40: keep \"thanks!\"\n- 240: \"thanks, that works!\"\n- 319: \"thanks a million!\"\n- 450: \"thanks, all good!\"\n- 523: \"thanks so much!\"\n\n- 39: keep \"that would help.\"\n- 182: \"that would help a ton.\"\n- 284: \"that would help, honestly.\"\n- 344: \"that would help if you can.\"\n- 424: \"that would help me a lot.\"\n- 501: \"that would help with the shadows.\"\n- 594: \"that would help, thanks.\"\n\nCheck none of these already exist in file: \"that would help a lot.\" exists (line ~573 area, \"that would help a lot, thanks!\" also exists). My variants are distinct. \"thanks a lot!\" exists; \"thanks a million!\" fine.\n\nUse sed -i with line numbers.",
